Aisha (SE): Quick heads-up: Tomorrow's POC with MeridianBank — they want to run a high-concurrency demo using a single WebSocket connection (multiplexed sessions) from our Rust client. Requirements: strictly-validated JSON responses (schema-driven), deterministic function-like outputs for downstream reconciliation, and minimal log retention. Any gotchas before I send the runbook?

Tom (AE): Good to know. Pricing note: sustained 400 concurrent sessions likely favors Dedicated; I'll prepare a ballpark. Do they expect burst or steady-state?

Aisha (SE): They said steady for the afternoon window (2 hours). Also asked: can we guarantee no PII in logs during the demo?

Nina (Solutions): For structured output: two paths — 1) use our function-calling interface with an explicit JSON schema so the model returns typed fields, or 2) a strict response-sanitizer in the client that validates and requests a deterministic re-run if the model strays. For the demo I'd lean function-calling + temp=0 to lower variance.

Raj (Runtime): On multiplexing: supported but session isolation is critical. Add an `x-session-id` and `x-tenant` header per logical chat. Server-side KV caching is keyed by session id; without it you'll see cross-session cache contamination. Also throttle inflight streams per connection (we suggest 6-10).

Aisha (SE): Can you paste a minimal Rust snippet for the demo pattern?

Nina (Solutions): Pseudo-example (keep short):

```rust
let c = Redwood::with_key("...");
let ws = c.websocket("/v1/stream");
ws.add_header("x-tenant", tenant);
ws.add_header("x-session-id", session_id);
ws.set_max_inflight(8);
ws.on_chunk(|chunk| {
  if !validate_json_schema(&chunk, schema) { ws.request_replay(session_id); }
});
```

Aisha (SE): Nice. Raj, anything about backpressure or large transcripts? Meridian will pipe multi-page statements, some >10k tokens.

Raj (Runtime): Large contexts: prefer KV prefix caching for shared system prompts and truncate or summarize user history on the client before sending. Also increase `max_batch_tokens` carefully; otherwise the server queues will build up and you'll get tail latency. If the client experiences stalls, drop `max_inflight` or enable Dedicated burst capacity.

Tom (AE): For cost sketch — Dedicated with a small reserved footprint + burst covers that steady 2-hour window cheaper than hosted metered for sustained throughput. I'll attach a pros/cons slide.

Aisha (SE): Security Q: how to answer "where are request logs stored and for how long" for the POC? They want an immediate reply.

Nina (Solutions): Short presales reply: "Default debug retention is 30 days for hosted POCs; for sensitive POCs we can enable shorter retention or opt-out. Data at rest is encrypted; Private deployments can use customer KMS. We'll escalate their formal questionnaire to Security for a full response." (avoid specifics here).

Aisha (SE): Ops-bot, can you spin a quick canary for 20 multiplexed sessions so we can demo stability?

ops-bot: Canary request accepted: demo-multiplex-20 — started 2112345730. Dashboard: http://internal/ci/2112345730

Raj (Runtime): I'll monitor cache hit rates and inflight queue length. If schema fail rate >3% enable client-side validation + retry. For deterministic outputs set temperature=0 and lower top_p. Also log schema failures (not full user text) for debugging.

Tom (AE): I'll join the call to discuss Dedicated options and bring the pricing sketch. Aisha, want me to ping Meridian with the runbook link?

Aisha (SE): Yes please, send the runbook and a note about the short retention option. Thanks all. :thumbsup:
